  "query_preview": "..\n\nSurface low pressure is centered over the Ohio Valley this morning \nwith a trailing hybrid inverted trough/cold front draped \nnorthwestward across the Thumb into northern Lower MI. As the low \nshifts east today, the trough will swing southward across SE MI with \nsynoptic support for ascent from an amplified mid-level shortwave \npassing overhead. Scattered to numerous rain showers accompany the \ntrough with gusty north to northeast wind (~25-30 mph) ensuing \nbehind it. It will be a rather raw day as temperatures reach the mid \n40s but the wind keeps the wind chill near or below 40 F. Cold air \nadvection gains traction tonight with 850 mb temps falling to around \n-6 C by Friday morning. This will be the driver behind an early lake \neffect precip event tonight into the late week. Showers will \ninitially be cold rain but transition to mainly melting snow \novernight.\n\nLake effect showers quickly become organized within the cold NNE \nflow over Lake Huron this evening, with models continuing to \nadvertise two dominant convergent bands - one in the western/central \nThumb and one along the eastern shoreline of the Thumb into the Port \nHuron vicinity. These will be fueled by long fetch over near record \nwarm Lake Huron temps still in the mid 50s F, resulting in sfc-850mb \ndelta-T of nearly 20 C. This contributes to impressive lake induced \nCAPE of 400 to 700 J/kg with the modified sounding showing \nconvective depth exceeding 10 kft and favorable intersection with \nthe DGZ, indicating efficient snowflake production and high precip \nrates overnight into midday Friday. The warmth of the lake will end \nup being a mitigating factor for snow accumulation, especially along \nthe lakeshore where wet bulb temps struggle to fall below the mid \n30s. A few miles inland however, the ambient temperature and dew \npoints tenuously near freezing will allow for minor slushy \naccumulations throughout portions of the Thumb Friday morning. \nLatest ensemble output continues to target areas downwind of the \ndominant band in eastern Sanilac and St. Clair Counties for \nlocalized amounts up to 1 to 2\". Low confidence exists for diabatic \ncooling within the most vigorous precip rates to lower column temps \nby a few degrees, leaving the door open for local overachievement in \nthis area.\n\nDaytime Friday will be brisk with wind chill holding in the 30s.\nSubtle backing to wind direction causes the lake effect bands to \nshift mainly offshore by late Friday afternoon. However, the \nimmediate eastern shoreline of the Thumb remains a target for \nadditional rain/snow into Friday night. Elsewhere, dry and cold \nconditions carry temperatures down into the 20s by early Saturday \nmorning. Cool cyclonic flow holds through Saturday but as the \nthermal trough begins to ease out, any remaining vigor to lake \neffect ends and temperatures rebound toward the mid 40s. A highly \namplified ridge then builds into the Great Lakes Sunday into Monday, \noffering dry and stable conditions. By Tuesday surface-850mb high \npressure reaches the Ohio Valley and burgeoning southwest flow \nbrings a steady warming trend through the middle of the week.",
